course: dictionary
lesson: 14592
title: Spotting a ploy
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A ploy is a clever trick or plan. People use a ploy to get an advantage, or to make someone do something they might not otherwise do. «The shop put a free gift by the till, but it was just a ploy to make us spend more.» The gift is not really free. It is a plan to get you to buy more.
explain: Ploys are often sneaky, so you have to look carefully. In sport, a team might pretend to kick one way and then pass the other way. That is a ploy to fool the other side. «The striker pretended to shoot, then passed the ball to his friend. It was a clever ploy.»
explain: To spot a ploy, ask yourself: what does this person want from me, and why are they being so nice? If the answer is that they want you to do something, you may be looking at a ploy. Adverts often use ploys, so stop and think before you buy.
fact: A ploy is a noun. One ploy, two ploys.
fact: «Ploy» means a clever trick or plan used to get an advantage or to make someone do something.
fact: In sport, a ploy is a tactic that tricks the other team.
fact: Marketing ploys, like a "free gift" that makes you buy more, are taught in media literacy lessons.
fact: A ploy is often sneaky, so it is good to check what someone really wants from you.
exercise: «The free gift at the till was a ploy to make us buy more.» What is a ploy here? | A present we did not ask for | A rule about paying at the till | *A clever plan to get us to spend more | A kind thing done for no reason
exercise: «The striker pretended to shoot, then passed the ball. It was a clever ploy.» What is a ploy here? | A pair of football boots | A rule about how the ball is kicked | *A clever trick to fool the other team
exercise: Which sentence shows a ploy? | Tom cleaned his room on Sunday. | Tom likes reading books about dinosaurs. | *Tom offered his sister sweets so she would tidy his room.
test: «The advert said, "Hurry, only one left!" It was a ploy to make us buy quickly.» Why would the advert use this ploy? | To help you save money | To tell you the shop is closing soon | *To make you buy quickly, before you stop to think
test: Which word means about the same as ploy? | Puddle | *Scheme | Whistle | Sunshine
exam: Sam says, «Mum is offering extra pocket money, but I think it's a ploy to get me to do the dishes.» What does Sam think a ploy is here? | To give Sam a rest from chores | To help Sam save for a bike | *A plan to make Sam do something, like the dishes | To pay Sam back for the dishes
